{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2452","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2452","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2452\",\"parent_revision\":\"c9ea564ea161a89e3ad44446bd099bdc0dda1c464b06697c87fd27a0912a1c8c\",\"root_revision\":\"c9ea564ea161a89e3ad44446bd099bdc0dda1c464b06697c87fd27a0912a1c8c\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nTwo related failures observed during the #2437 grooming session on 2026-05-24:\\n\\n**Failure 1 \u2014 stale reads when connected**\\n\\nbacklog_view(summary=True) reads from the local YAML cache file. When subagents write groomed content via backlog_groom, those writes sync to GitHub but the local YAML cache is not updated. Subsequent backlog_view(summary=True) calls return a sections_index reflecting only the first groomer's writes \u2014 not the swarm agents' writes. This happened even after calling backlog_pull. The server was connected to GitHub (backend availability: reachable) throughout.\\n\\nWhen a live connection to the backend host is available, the server must not return stale local cache data. A connected backend is the authoritative source; the local cache must be treated as write-through, not read-first.\\n\\n**Failure 2 \u2014 backlog_sync runs full network sweep instead of local flush**\\n\\nfinally.md documents backlog_sync(flush_only=true) as the groom workflow exit step. The loaded tool schema for backlog_sync shows one parameter: dry_run (bool). No flush_only parameter. Calling backlog_sync() with no arguments ran a full network sync: fetched 321 GitHub issues, updated 190, created 6. This happened at the end of every groom finalization. The finally.md document references a capability (flush_only) that the tool does not expose, causing every groom to trigger an expensive bulk operation.\\n\\nThese two failures share a root cause: the local cache layer is not coherent with the backend when the backend is reachable. The fix requires the server to: (1) read from the live backend when connected rather than from local cache, and (2) either implement flush_only or update finally.md to reflect the actual tool contract.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: backlog_view(summary=True) reads from local YAML cache rather than live GitHub when connected\\nevidence: operations.py::view_item calls parse_backlog() to seed from local YAML, then view_enrich_from_github() enriches only raw body \u2014 NOT result.sections or result.sections_index\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py\\n\\nverdict: REFUTED\\nclaim: backlog_groom writes sync to GitHub but does NOT update the local YAML cache file afterward\\nevidence: Code inspection shows _handle_update_groomed writes to local cache file AFTER GitHub sync (Step 2 in the write path). The stale sections_index is a concurrent write race: each groom call does read-modify-write to local YAML; parallel agents' writes clobber each other's sections in the local file even though GitHub receives all sections independently.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: backlog_pull does not fix the stale cache issue (subsequent backlog_view still returns stale data after pull)\\nevidence: pull_by_selector (operations.py:3768) exists and calls _pull_item (operations.py:1611). Whether a fresh pull re-seeds sections from GitHub body correctly requires runtime testing \u2014 could not be determined from static analysis alone.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1611,3768\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: The backlog_sync MCP tool schema has only a dry_run parameter \u2014 no flush_only parameter exists\\nevidence: backlog_sync in server.py exposes only dry_run: bool. sync_items in operations.py has no flush_only parameter \u2014 always runs the full two-phase sweep.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py, plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: finally.md references backlog_sync(flush_only=true) but this parameter does not exist in the actual tool schema\\nevidence: finally.md documents backlog_sync(flush_only=true) as the groom workflow exit step. Confirmed: no flush_only parameter exists in backlog_sync tool or sync_items function.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: backlog MCP server returns stale local cache when live GitHub connection is available, and backlog_sync runs full network sweep instead of local flush\\nDate: 2026-05-24\\nGoal: Fix the backlog MCP server so that backlog_view reads live GitHub section data when connected (not stale local YAML), and backlog_sync either exposes flush_only or finally.md is corrected to match actual tool contract.\\nConditions:\\n1. backlog_view(summary=True) reads from local YAML cache even when GitHub is reachable | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Claim 1 VERIFIED \u2014 operations.py::view_item calls parse_backlog (local YAML seed) then view_enrich_from_github enriches raw body only, not sections\\n2. backlog_groom writes do NOT update local YAML cache (original claim) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: MISSING (REFUTED) | Citation: Claim 2 REFUTED by fact-checker \u2014 backlog_groom DOES write local YAML via read-modify-write after GitHub sync; actual mechanism is concurrent write race\\n3. backlog_pull does not fix stale cache | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Citation: Claim 3 INCONCLUSIVE \u2014 pull_by_selector (operations.py:3768) + _pull_item (operations.py:1611) exist; runtime behavior unconfirmed\\n4. backlog_sync schema has only dry_run \u2014 no flush_only | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Claim 4 VERIFIED \u2014 server.py::backlog_sync and operations.py::sync_items confirmed\\n5. finally.md references flush_only=true which does not exist | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Claim 5 VERIFIED \u2014 finally.md and tool schema confirmed mismatch\\n6. Desired behavior: flush_only = pending local changes synced without full sweep | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE\\n7. Files implementing backlog_view read path | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 operations.py::view_item, parse_backlog, view_enrich_from_github confirmed\\n8. Files implementing backlog_sync | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 server.py::backlog_sync + operations.py::sync_items confirmed\\n9. Backend connectivity detection exists | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Bash grep server.py:1283 \u2014 \\\"Backend availability: reachable\\\" state already tracked\\n10. Targeted single-item pull exists | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Bash grep \u2014 operations.py:1611 _pull_item, operations.py:3768 pull_by_selector confirmed\\n11. view_enrich_from_github enriches raw body only \u2014 NOT sections/sections_index | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 code inspection of view_item call chain\\n12. Concurrent write race: parallel backlog_groom calls use read-modify-write \u2192 last-writer-wins in local YAML | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst + fact-checker (Claim 2 refutation reveals actual mechanism)\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 2: AVAILABLE \u2192 MISSING (REFUTED by fact-checker; original claim was wrong)\\n- Condition 3: AVAILABLE \u2192 DERIVABLE (INCONCLUSIVE \u2014 needs runtime test)\\n- Conditions 7-10: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (self-resolved by impact-analyst + Bash grep)\\n- Conditions 11-12: (new) AVAILABLE (discovered by impact-analyst + fact-check refutation)\\nMISSING resolution: Condition 2 (original claim) is REFUTED \u2014 the actual mechanism is a concurrent write race. This changes the root cause but not the observable symptom. The fix path is unchanged: backlog_view must read live sections when connected. Condition 2 MISSING is self-resolved by understanding the corrected mechanism (condition 12).\\nAVAILABLE count: 10\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix: backlog mcp server returns stale local cache when live github connection is available, and backlog_sync runs full network sweep instead of local flush** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 work-backlog-item #2437 grooming swarm 2026-05-24\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-25)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n**Failure 1 \u2014 stale sections_index after concurrent writes:**\\n\\n1. Start a grooming swarm with multiple parallel subagents, each assigned to groom a distinct section of the same backlog item.\\n2. Each agent calls `backlog_groom(selector=\\\"#N\\\", section=\\\"Section A\\\", content=\\\"...\\\")` \u2014 this syncs to GitHub and updates the local YAML cache.\\n3. After all agents complete, call `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#N\\\", summary=True)` from any agent context.\\n4. Observe: `sections_index` reflects only the sections written by the last agent to write. Sections written by earlier agents are absent or show pre-groom content.\\n5. Call `backlog_pull(selector=\\\"#N\\\")` to attempt refresh, then repeat `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#N\\\", summary=True)`.\\n6. Observe: sections_index may still be stale \u2014 the enrichment path reads from local YAML rather than live GitHub sections.\\n\\n**Failure 2 \u2014 backlog_sync triggers full network sweep:**\\n\\n1. A groom workflow calls `backlog_sync()` with no arguments at finalization (as referenced in `finally.md` via `flush_only=true`).\\n2. Observe: the actual tool schema has no `flush_only` parameter \u2014 `flush_only=True` is silently ignored or raises a parameter error.\\n3. `backlog_sync()` with no arguments runs a full 321-issue sweep across all GitHub issues.\\n4. Confirm by inspecting the tool schema: `backlog_sync` exposes only `dry_run` (bool), not `flush_only`.\\n\\n**Minimal reproduction for Failure 2 (schema check):**\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_sync --help   # or inspect MCP tool schema\\n# Expected: flush_only parameter listed\\n# Actual: only dry_run is present\\n```\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**P1 \u2014 Should-have fix with active user impact**\\n\\nJustification:\\n- Failure 1 (stale cache) is directly observable in production grooming swarms \u2014 the issue was discovered during a live #2437 grooming session on 2026-05-24. Any multi-agent backlog workflow produces incorrect sections_index for any caller that reads the item after concurrent writes. This is not a theoretical race condition: it was observed and the root cause is confirmed.\\n- Failure 2 (full sweep instead of flush) causes every groom finalization to trigger a 321-issue network sweep. At scale, this degrades throughput of grooming workflows and burns GitHub API rate limit budget unnecessarily.\\n- Both failures compound: Failure 1 leaves the cache in a state that Failure 2's full sweep is supposed to correct, but Failure 2 is too expensive to call as a remedy.\\n\\nRisk of not fixing: Every parallel grooming swarm produces unreliable sections_index. Orchestrators relying on summary=True to route follow-up work will act on stale data. The finally.md agent instruction references a non-existent tool parameter, meaning the intended flush behavior is silently never executed.\\n\\nEffort: **M (1\u20133 days)**\\n- Failure 2 fix-path B (update finally.md only): < 1 day\\n- Failure 2 fix-path A (implement flush_only): 1\u20132 days (touches server.py, operations.py, backend_protocol.py, 4 backend impls)\\n- Failure 1 fix (enrich sections_index from live GitHub or reorder local write logic): 1\u20132 days\\n- Test coverage for both paths: 0.5 day\\n- Combined: 1\u20133 days depending on fix-path chosen for Failure 2\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n**Failure 1 \u2014 stale sections_index:**\\n\\n- Blocks: any orchestrator or agent that uses `backlog_view(summary=True)` after concurrent grooming writes to determine which sections exist, which are complete, or which need follow-up. The sections_index it receives omits or misrepresents sections written by non-last writers.\\n- Affected workflows: all multi-agent grooming swarms where more than one agent writes to the same backlog item concurrently (e.g., grooming swarms spawning one agent per section).\\n- Affected agents: `agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` and any orchestrator reading sections_index to route post-groom work.\\n- Race condition scope: the local YAML write in backlog_groom reads-modifies-writes the full item file. When two agents do this concurrently, the second write overwrites the first agent's section in the local file, even though both wrote their sections to GitHub independently. The local YAML becomes a last-writer-wins snapshot that does not reflect the GitHub state.\\n- Severity: data visible on GitHub is correct; data returned by `backlog_view` from local cache is incomplete. Agents acting on `backlog_view` results operate on incorrect information.\\n\\n**Failure 2 \u2014 full network sweep on groom finalization:**\\n\\n- Bottleneck: every groom finalization triggers a 321-issue GitHub sweep, consuming API rate limit and adding latency for every groomed item.\\n- Broken contract: `finally.md` instructs agents to call `backlog_sync(flush_only=True)`. This parameter does not exist in the tool schema. The instruction is silently non-functional.\\n- Affected agents: all agents following the groom finalization workflow via `finally.md`.\\n- Cascading effect: the sweep that was intended to be a local flush becomes a full crawl. In a grooming swarm processing multiple items, this compounds with each item finalized.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Multi-agent grooming swarms produce reliable sections_index that reflects all concurrent writes, not just the last writer's snapshot.\\n- Orchestrators can use `backlog_view(summary=True)` after concurrent grooming and receive accurate section metadata for routing follow-up work.\\n- Groom finalization no longer triggers a 321-issue network sweep \u2014 API rate limit budget is consumed proportionally to the work done.\\n- The `finally.md` agent instruction becomes executable \u2014 agents following it will perform the intended finalization behavior rather than silently calling a no-op parameter.\\n- Test coverage for the concurrent-write path and the sync behavior makes both failure modes regressionable \u2014 future changes to the groom/sync pipeline cannot silently reintroduce either failure.\\n- Confidence in backlog_view as a reliable read operation increases for all callers, including orchestrators making routing decisions based on sections_index.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n**Failure 1 \u2014 sections_index accuracy:**\\n\\nWhen multiple agents write to different sections of the same backlog item concurrently via `backlog_groom`, and a subsequent caller invokes `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#N\\\", summary=True)`, the returned `sections_index` must reflect all sections that were written \u2014 not only the sections written by the last agent to update the local cache. The read-after-write contract must hold: any section visible on GitHub must appear in `sections_index` when GitHub connectivity is available.\\n\\n**Failure 2 \u2014 backlog_sync scope:**\\n\\nWhen a groom finalization workflow needs to flush local state to GitHub without performing a full network sweep of all issues, the MCP tool interface must provide a way to do this. The `finally.md` workflow instruction must accurately describe the tool contract available to agents. If a `flush_only` mode is not implemented, `finally.md` must not reference it; if it is implemented, the tool schema must expose it.\\n\\nIn either case: calling the groom finalization path must not trigger a full sweep of 321 GitHub issues as a side effect. The cost of finalization must be proportional to the scope of the work done (one item, not all items).\\n\\n**Combined \u2014 stable read semantics for concurrent workflows:**\\n\\nA backlog item touched by a grooming swarm must be readable by any subsequent caller with accurate section metadata, without requiring the caller to know which agent wrote last or to call a repair operation. The backlog system must handle concurrent section writes without requiring a serialization guarantee at the caller level.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given a backlog item where two or more agents have written distinct sections via `backlog_groom` concurrently, `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#N\\\", summary=True)` returns a `sections_index` that includes all written sections \u2014 not only the sections from the last writer.\\n\\n2. Given a live GitHub connection is available, `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#N\\\", summary=True)` returns section metadata that matches the current GitHub issue body \u2014 not the local YAML snapshot from the previous read.\\n\\n3. Given a groom finalization workflow executes the path described in `finally.md`, the operation does not trigger a full sweep of all GitHub issues. The GitHub API call count during finalization of a single item must be bounded to operations on that item, not across all items.\\n\\n4. Given `finally.md` references a `backlog_sync` parameter (currently `flush_only=true`), the MCP tool schema for `backlog_sync` must expose that parameter with matching behavior \u2014 OR `finally.md` must document the actual tool contract accurately so agents following it do not call a non-existent parameter.\\n\\n5. Given a test exercises the concurrent-write path (two groom writes to different sections of the same item without serialization), the test asserts that a subsequent `backlog_view(summary=True)` returns all written sections. This test must exist in `backlog_core/tests/` and must fail before the fix and pass after.\\n\\n6. Given a test exercises the `backlog_sync` scope behavior, the test asserts that the finalization path does not invoke a full-sweep code path unless explicitly requested. This test must exist in `backlog_core/tests/` and must fail before the fix and pass after.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Code | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 view_item, sync_items |\\n| Code | `plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_backlog_server.py` \u2014 backlog_sync tool definition |\\n| Code | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 BacklogBackend protocol |\\n| Workflow doc | `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md` |\\n| Agent | `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` |\\n| Tests | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` |\\n| Fact-check | Fact-checker agent output, session 2026-05-24 \u2014 VERIFIED/REFUTED/INCONCLUSIVE verdicts documented in item description |\\n| Impact analysis | Impact-analyst output, session 2026-05-24 \u2014 blast radius and fix-path decision framing |\\n| Backlog | #2437 \u2014 grooming swarm where both failures were first observed |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nFiles identified as within the impact radius (from impact-analyst):\\n\\n**Failure 1 \u2014 stale cache read path:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `view_item` function calls `parse_backlog()` then `view_enrich_from_github()`; enrichment updates raw body but not `result.sections` or `result.sections_index`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `sync_items` function (read-write path for local YAML)\\n\\n**Failure 2 \u2014 backlog_sync scope:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/run_backlog_server.py` (or equivalent server.py) \u2014 `backlog_sync` tool definition, exposes only `dry_run`, not `flush_only`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `sync_items` implementation; full sweep logic lives here\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 BacklogBackend protocol definition; any new method must be added here for all backends\\n- Backend implementations (all 4 require changes if fix-path A is chosen):\\n  - GitHub backend\\n  - SQLite backend\\n  - InMemory backend\\n  - Beads backend (if applicable)\\n\\n**Agent instruction:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md` \u2014 references `backlog_sync(flush_only=True)` which does not exist in tool schema\\n\\n**Agent file:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 executes finally.md; triggers bulk sweep on every groom cycle\\n\\n**Tests (zero coverage currently):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` \u2014 no existing tests for concurrent-write race or sync scope behavior\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 both failures are independently reproducible and fixable; no upstream blockers identified\\n- Blocks: any grooming swarm workflow that uses multi-agent concurrent section writes and relies on accurate sections_index post-completion; any grooming finalization that expects flush_only semantics\\n- Related: #2437 \u2014 the grooming swarm for this item was the first context where both failures were observed; fixing this item improves reliability for all future grooming swarms including re-grooming of #2437 if needed\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n**Estimate: M (1\u20133 days)**\\n\\nBreakdown:\\n- Failure 1 (stale sections_index): sections_index enrichment from live GitHub or concurrent-write protection in local YAML write path \u2014 estimated 1\u20132 days; the fix scope depends on whether the solution enriches the view path or corrects the write path; either touches operations.py and possibly the backend protocol\\n- Failure 2, fix-path B (update finally.md only): < 0.5 day; docs-only change that accurately reflects the actual tool contract; does not add flush semantics\\n- Failure 2, fix-path A (implement flush_only parameter): 1\u20132 days; touches server.py/run_backlog_server.py, operations.py, backend_protocol.py, and all backend implementations; integration testing required across backends\\n- Test coverage for both failure paths: 0.5 day; new tests in backlog_core/tests/\\n\\nTotal range: 1.5\u20134.5 days depending on fix-path chosen for Failure 2. Classified as M because fix-path B for Failure 2 is available and most of the complexity lives in Failure 1 and test coverage.\\n\\nUnknowns that may expand effort:\\n- Whether backlog_pull is sufficient for cache refresh (marked INCONCLUSIVE by fact-checker \u2014 requires runtime testing)\\n- Concurrent-write behavior across all 4 backend implementations (may expose per-backend variations)\\n- Whether existing tests can be extended or require new fixtures for concurrent-write scenarios\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 groomer agent that executes the groom workflow including the `finally.md` exit steps. Calls `backlog_sync(flush_only=true)` as instructed by `finally.md`. This agent will trigger the same Failure 2 (full network sweep) until `finally.md` is corrected or `flush_only` is implemented. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Agent directly executes the broken instruction; every groom swarm session triggers a bulk network sweep as a result.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/alignment-analyst.md` \u2014 calls `backlog_view(selector=selector, summary=False)` as its first step. Not directly affected by Failure 1 since it uses `summary=False`. However, if the fix changes the live-read contract, all agents calling `backlog_view` benefit from the fix without code changes. | Risk: LOW | Why: Correct usage pattern; not directly broken, benefits from fix.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/rtica-assessor.md` \u2014 calls `backlog_view(selector, summary=False)`. Same as alignment-analyst: not affected by the stale `sections_index` bug (which is a `summary=True` symptom), but benefits from improved cache coherence. | Risk: LOW | Why: Uses `summary=False`; not in the primary failure path.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/classifier.md` \u2014 calls `backlog_view(selector=item_ref, summary=False)`. Same pattern. | Risk: LOW | Why: Uses `summary=False`; not in the primary failure path.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` (this agent) \u2014 calls `backlog_view(selector=selector, summary=False)`. Follows the `finally.md` workflow including `backlog_sync(flush_only=true)` as exit step. Subject to Failure 2 at groom exit. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Directly executes the broken `flush_only` instruction through `finally.md`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/groom-check.md` \u2014 instructs callers to call `backlog_view` again after re-groom completes to retrieve fresh sections (line 76, line 131). This instruction is correct intent but fails in practice when `backlog_view(summary=True)` returns stale `sections_index` from local YAML. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The \\\"call backlog_view again to get fresh sections\\\" instruction is semantically correct but operationally broken until Failure 1 is fixed; agents following this step get stale data.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/issue-first.md` \u2014 states \\\"The `backlog_view` MCP tool merges local cache with live GitHub issue data\\\" (line 24). This claim is partially false for YAML items: `view_enrich_from_github` enriches the raw body but not the structured sections. This claim becomes accurate only after Failure 1 is fixed. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI-facing instruction that teaches agents an incorrect contract; agents relying on this claim will not call `backlog_pull` before relying on sections data.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 executes finally.md; currently triggers full 321-issue network sweep on every groom exit\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` \u2014 one of four backend implementations behind the `BacklogBackend` Protocol. If Fix path A (implement `flush_only`) requires backend-level changes, this file must implement a `flush_only_sync` or equivalent method. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Backend Protocol change propagates to all 4 backend implementations; github_backend is the production path.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` \u2014 beads backend implementation. Same migration obligation as github_backend if the `BacklogBackend` Protocol is extended with a `flush_only` method. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Protocol extension requires all implementations \u2014 even non-active ones \u2014 to implement the method or raise `BackendUnsupportedError`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py` \u2014 SQLite backend implementation. Same migration obligation. | Risk: LOW | Why: Not the primary production backend; Protocol extension still requires an implementation or `NotImplementedError`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py` \u2014 in-memory test double. Same migration obligation if Protocol is extended. | Risk: LOW | Why: Test-double; no production path, but test isolation depends on this implementing the same interface.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py::BacklogBackend` \u2014 Protocol interface that all backends implement. If `flush_only` requires a new Protocol method, this file is the definition point. All four backend files then become consumers. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Protocol extension is a breaking change across all implementations; must be additive (default impl or `Optional`) to avoid forced migration on all backends.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::sync_create_missing_issues` \u2014 called by `sync_items`. If `flush_only` is implemented, this function is NOT called in `flush_only` mode. The function boundary and contract stay unchanged. | Risk: LOW | Why: Not directly changed, but its selective exclusion by `flush_only` must be correct.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::sync_push_groomed_content` \u2014 called by `sync_items`. In `flush_only` mode this is either called exclusively (push pending local writes without pulling) or replaced by a narrower write path. | Risk: LOW | Why: Same as `sync_create_missing_issues`; the semantics of `flush_only` determine which of these is called.\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` \u2014 reads local cache via `parse_backlog()` then conditionally calls `view_enrich_from_github()` for the raw body, but YAML items populate `result.sections` and `result.sections_index` from the in-memory `BacklogItem` parsed from the local YAML file \u2014 never refreshed from GitHub. Fix must either replace the `parse_backlog()` seed with a live GitHub read when connected, or call `_pull_item` inline before assembling sections. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Thi