y_selector` + `_pull_item` \u2014 targeted single-item pull already exists (operations.py:1611, 3768); may be the correct primitive for live-refresh\\n- All callers of `backlog_view(summary=True)` that depend on fresh sections_index\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` \u2014 if flush_only is added to sync, all 4 backends must implement it\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 reachable state already tracked (server.py:1283 \\\"Backend availability: reachable\\\")\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` \u2014 reads from local YAML cache via parse_backlog(); view_enrich_from_github enriches raw body only; needs to refresh sections from live GitHub when connected\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::sync_items` \u2014 runs full 2-phase network sweep; needs flush_only mode or caller needs updated contract\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_sync` \u2014 exposes only dry_run param; missing flush_only parameter\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/backlog-sync.yml` \u2014 calls `backlog_sync` with no arguments (`--target backlog_sync`, no `--input-json`). This invokes the default `dry_run=False` path \u2014 a full network sweep. If `flush_only` is added, this CI workflow is unaffected (callers not passing `flush_only` continue to get full sync behavior). No change needed for Fix path B either. | Risk: LOW | Why: Additive change (new optional parameter with default) is backward compatible; CI workflow continues to work correctly regardless of fix path.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: NOT_APPLICABLE \u2014 this is a bug fix, not a design change.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md` \u2014 documents `backlog_sync(flush_only=true)` as Step 1 of the groom exit sequence. The `flush_only` parameter does not exist in the tool schema. Fix path A: update this doc once `flush_only` is implemented (minimal change). Fix path B: update this doc to reflect the actual tool contract \u2014 either remove the step or replace with an alternative (e.g., `backlog_pull` on the current item). | Risk: HIGH | Why: This is the directly broken documentation that causes Failure 2 in every groom workflow execution; it is both wrong AND operationally expensive.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` \u2014 documents `backlog_sync` with `dry_run` as the only parameter (lines 93\u2013102). Accurate for the current schema. If Fix path A adds `flush_only`, this doc must be updated. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Primary reference for agents calling `backlog_sync`; a new parameter must be documented here to become discoverable.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/README.md` \u2014 documents `backlog_sync` at line 297 with `dry_run=False` as the only argument. Same update obligation as SKILL.md. Also contains the note at line 410 that \\\"backlog_sync and backlog_pull\\\" are the two write-direction tools. Behavioral description may need clarification on what `flush_only` does vs. a full sync. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI and human reference for the tool; stale after any parameter addition.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` \u2014 maps `backlog sync --dry-run` to `backlog_sync(dry_run=true)`. If `flush_only` is added, this migration table needs a new row for `backlog sync --flush-only` (if the CLI also gets the flag). | Risk: LOW | Why: Reference document; only matters if CLI and MCP both get `flush_only`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backend-providers.md` \u2014 documents `backlog_sync` as \\\"Bulk sync via `sync_issues_graphql`\\\" and describes the sync performance characteristics (full sync ~12s, incremental ~0.7s). If `flush_only` is a third mode, this doc needs a description of its performance characteristics and behavior. | Risk: LOW | Why: Architecture reference; accurate but incomplete after a new sync mode is added.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/artifact-conventions.md` \u2014 line 252 states \\\"backlog item cache is synced from GitHub Issues and can be refreshed via `backlog_sync`\\\". If the fix changes the primary cache coherence mechanism (e.g., live reads instead of sync-based refresh), this claim becomes misleading. | Risk: LOW | Why: Conceptual description; may need a sentence update if the cache model changes.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 line 359 describes `backlog_sync` as calling `operations.sync_items()`. Accurate for current code; update needed if the function signature or the sync path changes. | Risk: LOW | Why: Developer reference document; reflects implementation accurately until a code change makes it stale.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/README.md` \u2014 line 378 tables `backlog_sync` as \\\"Push local items to GitHub\\\". No parameter detail. Minimal update risk. | Risk: LOW | Why: Top-level description; single-line table entry unlikely to need updating unless the tool's purpose changes fundamentally.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md` \u2014 references backlog_sync(flush_only=true) which does not exist; MUST be updated regardless of fix path\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible (all 4 backends if Protocol extends)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added (cache coherence round-trip, sync_items flush_only, summary=True freshness)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated (finally.md \u2014 required; SKILL.md, README.md \u2014 if flush_only implemented)\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated (backlog-sync.yml \u2014 VERIFY_COMPATIBLE only)\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated (backlog-item-groomer.md, issue-first.md, groom-check.md)\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (Fix path A vs. B decision must be made before planning; blast radii differ significantly)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (pull_items merge semantic, view_enrich_from_github partial enrichment scope)\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type: defect**\\n\\nRationale: Both failures have traceable, identifiable cause chains from observable symptom to specific code behavior:\\n- Failure 1: `view_enrich_from_github()` enriches `raw body` but not `result.sections` or `result.sections_index` \u2192 stale sections_index after concurrent writes\\n- Failure 2: `finally.md` references `backlog_sync(flush_only=True)` \u2192 parameter does not exist in tool schema \u2192 full sweep runs instead of flush\\n\\nThese are not ambiguous design gaps, recurring patterns across subsystems, or missing guardrails. The code paths are identified, the behavior is verified, and the divergence from intended behavior is concrete and reproducible.\\n\\n**Analysis Method: 5-whys (Failure 1 \u2014 stale sections_index after concurrent writes)**\\n\\nSee `Root-Cause Analysis` section for the full 5-whys chain.\\n\\n**Scenario Target:** Multi-agent grooming swarm where two or more agents write to distinct sections of the same backlog item concurrently via `backlog_groom`, followed by a caller invoking `backlog_view(summary=True)` to obtain sections_index for routing decisions.\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` \u2014 direct root of Failure 1; sections_index populated from stale local YAML even when GitHub is reachable\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_sync` + `operations.py::sync_items` \u2014 direct root of Failure 2; tool schema lacks flush_only, every groom exit triggers full 321-issue sweep\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finally.md` \u2014 broken agent instruction propagates Failure 2 to every groomer agent execution\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/` \u2014 zero test coverage for both failure paths; fix ships without regression protection\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility: tool schema mismatch between server.py and finally.md documentation\\n  - behavioral: \\\"keep longer section\\\" merge in pull_items can block GitHub-fresh data even when pull is used as workaround\\n  - verification: no tests for cache coherence after groom write, no tests for summary=True freshness, no tests for flush_only\\n  - documentation: issue-first.md makes a false claim about backlog_view merging live data for all fields\\n  - scope: fix has two distinct paths (implement flush_only vs. update docs) with materially different blast radii; all four backend implementations are in scope for Fix path A\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The `_pull_item` \\\"keep longer section\\\" merge semantic is an independent behavioral risk not described in the original item; it can cause cache coherence failure even after pull is called correctly. This is Failure 1's secondary reinforcement mechanism.\\n  - `issue-first.md` contains a false claim about backlog_view contract that teaches incorrect agent behavior independently of this bug.\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method: 5-whys \u2014 Failure 1: stale sections_index after concurrent writes**\\n\\n**Symptom:** After multiple agents write to distinct sections via `backlog_groom` concurrently, `backlog_view(summary=True)` returns a sections_index that reflects only the sections from the last writer.\\n\\n**Why 1:** `backlog_view(summary=True)` reads `sections_index` from local YAML cache, not from live GitHub.\\n- Evidence: VERIFIED by fact-checker \u2014 `backlog_view` reads from local YAML; `view_item` in operations.py calls `parse_backlog()` to seed the item from local file.\\n\\n**Why 2:** The local YAML cache reflects only the state of the last writer.\\n- Evidence: Impact-analyst confirmed \u2014 `backlog_groom` performs a read-modify-write on the full local YAML file for each section write. When two agents do this concurrently, the second write reads the file (which does not yet contain the first agent's section), modifies it with its own section, and writes it back. The first agent's section is absent from the result.\\n\\n**Why 3:** `view_enrich_from_github()` does not repair the stale state.\\n- Evidence: Impact-analyst identified \u2014 `view_enrich_from_github()` enriches `result.raw body` only; it does not update `result.sections` or `result.sections_index`. So even after calling this enrichment function, sections_index remains derived from the stale local YAML parse.\\n\\n**Why 4:** There is no merge-based write in the local YAML update path.\\n- Evidence: Derivable from impact-analyst's description of the read-modify-write pattern \u2014 the write path does not read the current file, merge the new section with existing sections, then write. It replaces the full file with the caller's version of the item.\\n\\n**Why 5 (boundary of verified evidence):** The local YAML file is used as a last-writer-wins cache with no conflict detection.\\n- Status: DERIVABLE from the above chain, consistent with the observed symptom. Not independently verified at code level; marked as next investigation target for the implementer.\\n\\n**Root cause:** `backlog_groom` writes the full local YAML item file without merging concurrent writes from other agents. `backlog_view` reads sections_index from this file without refreshing it from GitHub's authoritative state for sections structure. The combination produces a last-writer-wins sections_index that misrepresents the true state of the item after concurrent grooming.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` | Role: producer | Connection: builds ViewItemResult from local cache via parse_backlog(); sections and sections_index come from local YAML, not GitHub | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'def view_item'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_build_compact_manifest` | Role: producer | Connection: assembles sections_index field for summary=True response; inherits stale data from view_item | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: '_build_compact_manifest'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_sections_index_from_result` | Role: producer | Connection: reads result.sections_index and result.sections to build index string; both populated from local YAML | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: '_sections_index_from_result'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::parse_backlog` | Role: producer | Connection: central cache-read primitive; called by view_item, list_items, sync_items, pull_items, close_item, and others | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'parse_backlog'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::sync_items` | Role: producer | Connection: implements backlog_sync; no flush_only param; always runs full two-phase network sweep | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'def sync_items'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_sync` | Role: producer | Connection: MCP tool wrapper; exposes only dry_run; schema mismatch with finally.md | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'async def backlog_sync'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::pull_items` | Role: producer | Connection: pull path; \\\"keep longer section\\\" merge blocks GitHub-fresh data when local section is longer |